  card="After deciding what to produce, the next question is how to produce it: which methods, resources and technologies to use, for example whether to automate or employ more labour. Producers must select the right mix of the factors of production: land, labour, capital and technology. Production can be labour-intensive (more workers, less machinery) or capital-intensive (more machines and technology, fewer workers). Agriculture and handicrafts usually rely more on labour, while steel and automobile manufacturing depend more on machinery. The choice depends on the cost of capital, the technology available, the nature of the product, the availability and relative cost of factors, and laws and regulations. A garment maker will rely more on labour if machines are expensive and shift to automation if they become affordable; customised clothes need skilled labour while mass-produced garments suit machines; cheap labour favours labour-intensive methods; labour laws or incentives for machinery also influence the decision.",
